IMPORT prelude

-- g4-interpretation.l4: the ambiguities of Articles 11 and 12 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 (group G4), in the R4 shape of the brief.
-- One fork point is one enumeration, with one constructor per reading; all the fork points are fields of ONE record, `Interpretation of Articles 11 and 12`,
-- each field TYPICALLY the reading this encoding takes. Every public decision of the group that depends on a fork takes that record as an ordinary GIVEN
-- and delegates by CONSIDER to one function per reading, so that each reading is callable and has its own test (g4-tests-forks.l4).
-- DECLAREs only: no rule and no #EVAL live here. The register of forks (readings, the text that licenses each, the reading taken and why, the
-- discriminating case) is FRAGMENT-G4.md; the ids F4.1 to F4.12 below are its ids.

§ `Interpretation of Articles 11 and 12`

§§ `F4.1 -- the twelve months preceding the application`

-- F4.1, Article 11(2)(e)(ii). Reading A is taken: "preceding" excludes the day of the application, and the twelve months run back from it by whole calendar months.
-- B includes the day of the application and so starts the day AFTER the anniversary. C reads "twelve months" as twelve whole calendar months before the month of the application.
-- (How a year is taken back from 29 February is a separate fork point, F4.6.)
-- src:310 | (ii) any payment made to any governor of the entity in the 12 months preceding the application, and if an Order under Article 13(9)(b) applies to the registered charity, a draft of the proposed statement under that Order,
@ref Article 11(2)(e)(ii) (src:310)
DECLARE `How the twelve months preceding an application are counted` IS ONE OF
    `the twelve months ending on the day before the application`
    `the twelve months ending on the day of the application`
    `the twelve whole calendar months before the month of the application`

§§ `F4.2 -- the date of the application`

-- F4.2, Articles 11(2)(e)(ii), 11(8)(a)(i) and 19(6). Reading A is taken: the date of "the application" is the day it is lodged. B: the day it becomes complete, because
-- Article 19(6) says an application is "not complete" until Article 19(5) is complied with. One fork point serves both periods that start from "the application":
-- the twelve months of P5 and the period an Order may set for the Commissioner's decision (P7).
-- src:496 | (6) An application for registration is not complete if the entity has not complied with paragraph (5).
@ref Article 19(6) (src:496)
DECLARE `Which day is the date of an application` IS ONE OF
    `the day the application is lodged`
    `the day the application becomes complete`

§§ `F4.3 -- an application that does not comply`

-- F4.3, Articles 11(4), 11(5) and 19(6). Reading A is taken: while the applicant has not complied with Article 11(2), the Regulations and Article 19(5), neither duty arises (Article 11(4)
-- begins "If the applicant complies"; Article 11(5) refuses only where the Commissioner is "not so satisfied"). B: non-compliance is itself a failure to be satisfied, so the Commissioner must refuse.
-- C: Article 11(5) read literally, on its own words "If not so satisfied", does not repeat the condition of compliance: the duty to refuse depends on the six matters alone.
-- src:319 | (4) If the applicant complies with paragraph (2), with any Regulations under paragraph (3), and with Article 19(5), the Commissioner must register the applicant as a charity if satisfied that –
-- src:328 | (5) If not so satisfied, the Commissioner must refuse to register the applicant.
@ref Article 11(4) and (5) (src:319) (src:328)
DECLARE `What an application that does not comply leads to` IS ONE OF
    `no duty to register or to refuse arises while the application does not comply`
    `the Commissioner must refuse an application that does not comply`
    `the duty to refuse depends on the six matters alone`

§§ `F4.4 -- the duty to register and the bars on granting`

-- F4.4, Articles 11(4), 19(9)(c) and 20(1)(c). Reading A is taken: a bar on granting the application (a reported fitness issue undetermined, or its order or notice not complied with, or a court order that the
-- entity must not be registered) displaces the duty of Article 11(4): the Commissioner must not register. B: Article 11(4) prevails once its six matters hold. C: the provisions conflict and the encoding refuses.
-- src:502 | (c) must not, in the case of an application for registration, grant that application until the issue has been determined and any resulting order or notice complied with;
-- src:517 | (c) an order that the entity must not be registered as a charity;
@ref Articles 19(9)(c) and 20(1)(c) (src:502) (src:517)
DECLARE `How the duty to register meets the bars on granting the application` IS ONE OF
    `a bar on granting the application displaces the duty to register`
    `the duty to register prevails once its six matters hold`
    `the duty to register and the bars conflict and the model refuses`

§§ `F4.5 -- is a formerly registered charity another registered charity`

-- F4.5, Articles 12(1)(a), 12(4) and 33(5)(b). Reading A is taken: Article 1 defines "registered charity" as an entity entered in the general or the restricted section, so an entity in the historic section
-- is not "any other registered charity". B: it counts.
-- src:34 | “registered charity” means an entity entered for the time being in the general section or the restricted section of the register;
-- src:355 | (a) is the same as, or too similar to, the name of any other registered charity;
@ref Articles 1 "registered charity" and 12(1)(a) (src:34) (src:355)
DECLARE `Whether a formerly registered charity is another registered charity` IS ONE OF
    `a formerly registered charity is not another registered charity`
    `a formerly registered charity is another registered charity`

§§ `F4.6 -- a year before 29 February`

-- F4.6, Article 11(2)(e)(ii). The twelve months are counted back from the date of the application by a year; when that date is 29 February, the same day one year earlier does not exist.
-- Reading A is taken: the daydate library's `add years`, which falls back to 28 February (the last day of the month, as Excel's EDATE does). B: the lenient `Date` constructor, which rolls forward to 1 March.
-- src:310 | (ii) any payment made to any governor of the entity in the 12 months preceding the application, and if an Order under Article 13(9)(b) applies to the registered charity, a draft of the proposed statement under that Order,
@ref Article 11(2)(e)(ii) (src:310)
DECLARE `How a year is taken back from 29 February` IS ONE OF
    `a year before 29 February is 28 February`
    `a year before 29 February is 1 March`

§§ `F4.7 -- which names of another charity are compared`

-- F4.7, Article 12(1)(a). "the name of any other registered charity". A (taken): every registered name of that charity, its registered name and any alternative registered name (Article 12(7)(a) speaks of
-- "its registered names", and Article 12(3) and (5) make an alternative registered name a registered name). B: only the registered name of Article 12(2)(a). C: every name entered for it in the register, a previous name included (Article 8(3)(l)).
-- src:355 | (a) is the same as, or too similar to, the name of any other registered charity;
@ref Article 12(1)(a) (src:355)
DECLARE `Which names of another registered charity Article 12(1)(a) compares` IS ONE OF
    `its registered name and its alternative registered names`
    `its registered name only`
    `every name entered for it in the register, previous names included`

§§ `F4.8 -- a previous registered name of the charity itself`

-- F4.8, Article 12(7)(a). "its registered name or one of its registered names". A (taken): the names that are registered now, the registered name and the alternative registered names; a name the charity has given up
-- (Article 8(3)(l) keeps it on the register as a previous name) is a different name that must pass sub-paragraph (b). B: a previous registered name is still one of its registered names.
-- src:367 | (a) its registered name or one of its registered names; or
@ref Article 12(7)(a) (src:367)
DECLARE `Whether a previous registered name is still one of the charity's registered names` IS ONE OF
    `a previous registered name is not one of its registered names`
    `a previous registered name is still one of its registered names`

§§ `F4.9 -- counting the period for the Commissioner's decision`

-- F4.9, Article 11(8)(a)(i). An Order may fix "the period within which the Commissioner must make a decision on an application". The length is an input (no Order is among the sources).
-- Reading A is taken: the period starts the day after the date of the application, so the last day is that date plus the length. B: the date of the application is the first day, so the last day is one earlier.
-- src:339 | (i) the period within which the Commissioner must make a decision on an application,
@ref Article 11(8)(a)(i) (src:339)
DECLARE `How the period for the Commissioner's decision is counted` IS ONE OF
    `the period starts the day after the date of the application`
    `the date of the application is the first day of the period`

§§ `F4.10 -- reasonable time and as soon as practicable, for the guidance`

-- F4.10, Article 11(6)(b) applying Articles 5(7) and 5(8). Set together with F2.4 of group G2, which reads the same words in Articles 5(7) and 5(8). Reading A is taken: whether the Commissioner provided the copy
-- "within a reasonable time", and the Minister laid it "as soon as practicable", is a determination the caller supplies; no number is invented. B: a number of days, supplied as an extra input of that reading only.
-- src:164 | (7) Within a reasonable time after issuing or amending guidance the Commissioner must provide to the Minister a copy of the issued or amended guidance and the report published under paragraph (6)(b).
-- src:165 | (8) The Minister must lay a copy of the guidance and report so provided before the States as soon as practicable after the Minister receives the guidance and report.
-- src:331 | (b) Articles 5(6) to (8) apply to that guidance as they apply to guidance under Article 5(4);
@ref Article 11(6)(b) and Articles 5(7), 5(8) (src:331) (src:164-165)
DECLARE `How reasonable time and as soon as practicable are read for the guidance` IS ONE OF
    `as a determination the caller supplies`
    `as a number of days from the event`

§§ `F4.11 -- the payee who is a governor`

-- F4.11, Article 11(2)(e)(ii). "any payment made to any governor of the entity": a governor at what time? Reading A is taken: a person who was a governor when the payment was made.
-- B: a person who is a governor on the date of the application (so a payment to a former governor is not reached, and a payment to someone who became a governor later is).
-- src:310 | (ii) any payment made to any governor of the entity in the 12 months preceding the application, and if an Order under Article 13(9)(b) applies to the registered charity, a draft of the proposed statement under that Order,
@ref Article 11(2)(e)(ii) (src:310)
DECLARE `Which payees Article 11(2)(e)(ii) reaches` IS ONE OF
    `a payee who was a governor when the payment was made`
    `a payee who is a governor on the date of the application`

§§ `F4.12 -- "applies or should be applied" in Article 11(4)(f)`

-- F4.12, Article 11(4)(f). "no other ground for refusal, prescribed under paragraph (7), applies or should be applied". Reading A is taken: a ground on which an application MUST be refused "applies", and a ground on which it MAY be
-- refused "should be applied" when the Commissioner so considers (Article 9(6)(c) says the same in more words), so that each word does work. B: any prescribed ground whose circumstances are present defeats the matter, whether or not
-- the Commissioner would apply it, because "applies" is not qualified.
-- src:327 | (f) no other ground for refusal, prescribed under paragraph (7), applies or should be applied.
@ref Article 11(4)(f) (src:327)
DECLARE `How applies or should be applied is read in Article 11(4)(f)` IS ONE OF
    `a ground of mandatory refusal applies, or a ground of discretionary refusal should be applied`
    `any prescribed ground whose circumstances are present defeats the matter`

§§ `The record of the readings`

-- The twelve fork points as the fields of one record. Each field is TYPICALLY the reading taken; a caller who leaves a field out gets that reading, and the answer lists it as presumed.
@ref Articles 11 and 12 (the twelve forks)
DECLARE `Interpretation of Articles 11 and 12` HAS
    `how the twelve months preceding the application are counted` IS A `How the twelve months preceding an application are counted`
        TYPICALLY `the twelve months ending on the day before the application`
    `which day is the date of the application` IS A `Which day is the date of an application`
        TYPICALLY `the day the application is lodged`
    `what an application that does not comply leads to` IS A `What an application that does not comply leads to`
        TYPICALLY `no duty to register or to refuse arises while the application does not comply`
    `how the duty to register meets the bars on granting the application` IS A `How the duty to register meets the bars on granting the application`
        TYPICALLY `a bar on granting the application displaces the duty to register`
    `whether a formerly registered charity is another registered charity` IS A `Whether a formerly registered charity is another registered charity`
        TYPICALLY `a formerly registered charity is not another registered charity`
    `how a year is taken back from 29 February` IS A `How a year is taken back from 29 February`
        TYPICALLY `a year before 29 February is 28 February`
    `which names of another registered charity Article 12(1)(a) compares` IS A `Which names of another registered charity Article 12(1)(a) compares`
        TYPICALLY `its registered name and its alternative registered names`
    `whether a previous registered name is still one of its registered names` IS A `Whether a previous registered name is still one of the charity's registered names`
        TYPICALLY `a previous registered name is not one of its registered names`
    `how the period for the Commissioner's decision is counted` IS A `How the period for the Commissioner's decision is counted`
        TYPICALLY `the period starts the day after the date of the application`
    `how reasonable time and as soon as practicable are read for the guidance` IS A `How reasonable time and as soon as practicable are read for the guidance`
        TYPICALLY `as a determination the caller supplies`
    `which payees Article 11(2)(e)(ii) reaches` IS A `Which payees Article 11(2)(e)(ii) reaches`
        TYPICALLY `a payee who was a governor when the payment was made`
    `how applies or should be applied is read in Article 11(4)(f)` IS A `How applies or should be applied is read in Article 11(4)(f)`
        TYPICALLY `a ground of mandatory refusal applies, or a ground of discretionary refusal should be applied`
